# RP-5.6: Human-in-the-Loop Testing for Artificial Intelligence-Native Languages

## Abstract
Automated tests and pure-domain fuzz tests find objective conditions effectively. However, they do not evaluate subjective quality well. Examples of subjective quality include user interface text, layout judgment, ethical rules, and security design. These decisions do not have a cheap test oracle. This paper presents a human-in-the-loop testing architecture for the openOODA programming language. The architecture introduces native functions that allow autonomous fuzzing loops to pause and request human approval. This gives authority to humans while maintaining active automation. This theoretical system provides a verifiable, secure, and robust methodology for subjective software testing.

## 1. Introduction
The artificial intelligence-native loop of the openOODA system writes code and makes changes faster than a human can write contracts. Current automated testing tools cannot evaluate subjective properties. Types and capabilities are verified by the compiler. Integers and strict domains can be fuzz tested easily. Full functional correctness requires expensive mathematical proofs. Subjective quality and policy edge cases usually require human judgment. 

Without native human-in-the-loop functions, development teams make ad-hoc tools. They use manual checklists or external communication platforms. These ad-hoc tools do not record as part of the formal test graph. They can block continuous integration systems indefinitely. They do not appear in package attestations. Furthermore, they encourage autonomous agents to skip human checks entirely. 

The theoretical openOODA system provides a formal testing pause point at the language level. This serves human reviewers, artificial intelligence fuzzers, and continuous integration systems. It prevents adversarial agents from faking approvals. This paper outlines the architecture, security models, and operational modes of this system.

## 2. Related Work
Software engineering research explores human-in-the-loop systems and interactive test tools. Metamorphic testing provides partial oracles when full oracles are not available. Crowdsourced testing addresses the oracle problem by distributing subjective reviews. Recent systems mix automated tests with human checks for trust and safety in machine learning models. 

Industry uses Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback. This trains models from human preference data. The openOODA human-in-the-loop testing shares analogies with this method. Both use human preference labels and expensive human blocks. However, the openOODA human-in-the-loop testing is a test oracle function. It is not a reinforcement learning training loop. The language does not use reinforcement learning internally.

## 3. Architecture and Methodology
The system introduces a native verification function into the programming language. This function shows a prompt and an artifact digest to a secure human channel. The channel can be a command-line interface, a desktop user interface, or a remote review application programming interface. The system stops the automated test task until a human gives a decision. The system also stops the task if a timeout occurs. 

The system records the human decision in a formal test trace during packaging. This trace is cryptographically signed or made append-only. Artificial intelligence agents must not call a hidden automatic approval function. The compiler enforces this separation strictly.

The security of the human-in-the-loop system uses capability-based security. Only an entity with a specific human attestation capability can approve a test. A continuous integration environment without a terminal must fail or skip the test by default. No silent pass is allowed. The system does not show secret artifacts to the human reviewer directly. It shows secure cryptographic hashes instead. Tests requiring human input are marked clearly. This keeps them separate from pure automated test suites unless a recorded replay is used.

The system operates in multiple theoretical modes. Interactive mode shows the prompt on the terminal for immediate feedback. Record mode saves human decisions to a secure file. Replay mode uses saved decisions in continuous integration. Deny mode fails closed if the system finds a human-in-the-loop function. Skip mode explicitly skips the function and marks the software build as degraded. 

Autonomous fuzz loops interact with these subjective tests smoothly. The fuzz loop runs until it needs human verification. The system quarantines the generated artifact. A human session then batches multiple prompts together. The system promotes the software corpus only after human approval.

## 4. Threat and Failure Model
This architecture prevents autonomous agents from marking subjective tests as a pass without a verifiable human channel. It prevents a silent continuous integration pass when a strict policy is not set. It prevents the loss of the audit trail for release-critical subjective tests. 

The design does not prevent careless humans from approving tests without reading them. It does not prevent reviewers who work together maliciously. It does not prevent the social engineering of reviewers with false prompts. It also does not prevent the misuse of human checks to replace missing automated contracts on objective conditions. 

If the continuous integration hangs forever, the system uses timeouts and deny mode. If saved results become old, the system removes the results when the artifact hash changes. To prevent prompt injection through the artifact, the system shows safe metadata only. It opens the raw artifact only when the reviewer explicitly requests it. To prevent the overuse of human checks, a lint tool limits the count per test suite.

## 5. Discussion
We considered process-only code review for subjective testing. This is necessary but misses the review of generated artifacts during the active test phase. Using an artificial intelligence as a judge is a useful aid. However, it is not a human function and has severe gaming risks. Removing subjective tests from the language forces ad-hoc tools. Autonomous agents will skip these ad-hoc tools. A full reinforcement learning stack in the compiler is out of scope. It operates at the wrong abstraction layer. Manual quality assurance alone is too slow for artificial intelligence-native development loops.

Open theoretical research questions remain. We must determine the smallest attestation format to show users which human checks ran. We must solve multi-reviewer agreement for high-risk software publishing. We must keep the system accessible and support multiple programming languages. We must address legal and privacy concerns regarding storing human notes next to software artifacts.

## 6. Conclusion
Human-in-the-loop testing provides a critical pause point in autonomous development loops. By integrating subjective test oracles directly into the language, openOODA ensures that human authority governs qualitative software properties. This maintains the speed of artificial intelligence generation while preserving trust and safety in the resulting systems.

## 7. References
1. P. Christiano et al., "Deep Reinforcement Learning from Human Preferences," 2017.
2. Software testing "oracle problem" surveys.

